<section class="space-y-6 leading-relaxed">
  <p class="border-primary border-l-4 py-2 pl-4 text-xl italic opacity-60">
    "I can only be fully myself when I know I'm welcome here."
  </p>

  <p>
    Team development isn't just about tuning into each other — it's about
    relating to each other with all the differences people bring: temperament,
    experience, language, attitude. Where integration provides orientation,
    differentiation opens space for individuality. Teams grow when not just the
    common ground is strong, but when individual expression has a place.
  </p>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">
    Informal Relationship Building
  </h2>

  <p>
    In a team, people often meet first through their functions: the project
    lead, the developer, the communications person. But lasting collaboration
    doesn't grow from clear responsibilities alone — it grows from trust between
    the people behind those titles.
  </p>

  <p>
    Informal relationship building is the path through which team members
    gradually get to know each other as whole people — with their own stories,
    perspectives, and quirks. This happens in the casual moments of daily life:
    the brief chat before a meeting, sharing a meal, shared laughter after a
    tough phase.
  </p>

  <p>
    Where informal spaces are cultivated, more than sympathy grows. A posture of
    mutual attentiveness develops — a willingness to tolerate misunderstandings,
    to not judge difference too quickly, to meet each other with genuine
    curiosity.
  </p>

  <p>
    This kind of connection takes time — not as an abstract value but as
    concrete practice. It emerges when teams consciously create opportunities
    for personal encounters, beyond agenda and pressure.
  </p>

  <div class="card-bordered my-8">
    <h3 class="mb-2 font-semibold">Connection in the game</h3>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-80">
      Levels 1–4 are rich with informal connection: walks together, shared
      meals, party games, thank-you notes. These aren't fluff — they build the
      relational fabric that makes everything else possible.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <h3 class="text-info mb-2 font-semibold">
      Ideas for spending time together
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-80">
      Cooperative board games, an escape room, going out to eat, cooking
      together, traveling together, volunteering together, a movie night, trivia
      games, walks or hiking.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="mt-10 mb-4 text-2xl font-semibold">Structured Interventions</h2>

  <p>
    Informal relationship building is the foundation. Structured interventions
    are the framework for successful differentiation. A team grows not only
    through how well it knows each other, but through how consciously it engages
    with its differences.
  </p>

  <p>
    These interventions aren't "therapy sessions" for emergencies. They are
    concrete opportunities for ongoing self-clarification, alignment, and
    learning. They create spaces where people meet not "somehow" but guided,
    conscious, and on equal footing.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 text-xl font-semibold">
    Perception and Self-Reflection
  </h3>

  <p>
    A core element of any structured team development is engaging with one's own
    perception. Typical interventions explore: How do I see myself in the team?
    How do I experience others? How do my own assumptions and patterns affect
    our interactions?
  </p>

  <p>
    The goal isn't just self-knowledge — it's the willingness to understand
    one's own perspective as one among many. These interventions foster
    awareness of the plurality of perspectives, helping the team see difference
    not as disruption but as a starting point for understanding.
  </p>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <h3 class="text-info mb-2 font-semibold">
      Tip: Personal User Manuals (Readmes)
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-80">
      Personal user manuals are short, self-written texts where team members
      describe how they tick — how they work, communicate, make decisions,
      handle stress, and what they expect from others. They serve as a personal
      "instruction manual" that helps colleagues understand each other. Format
      is free: bullet points, short paragraphs, or creative forms. They promote
      differentiation by making differences visible and accessible in a human,
      appreciative way.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 text-xl font-semibold">Communication and Feedback</h3>

  <p>
    This area is about transparency, expressiveness, and mutual resonance.
    Interventions focus on understanding communication styles, handling
    misunderstandings, and feedback as a learning practice. Communication
    models, feedback rules, and dialogue formats are common tools.
  </p>

  <p>
    What matters more than technique is the attitude with which people speak and
    listen. Feedback needs trust and clarity. Communication is always a mirror
    of the relationship: giving feedback expresses not just facts but also
    proximity, relevance, and appreciation.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 text-xl font-semibold">
    Decision-Making and Participation
  </h3>

  <p>
    Teams differ greatly in how they handle decisions: who decides? How binding
    is it? How fast or thorough? Interventions in this area bring transparency
    to decision processes and participation expectations.
  </p>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <h3 class="text-info mb-2 font-semibold">
      Tip: The spectrum of decision-making
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-80">Try out different approaches as a team:</p>
    <ol class="mt-2 ml-5 list-decimal space-y-1 text-sm opacity-80">
      <li>Leader/Expert decides alone</li>
      <li>Individual decision with consultation (advice model)</li>
      <li>Majority decision</li>
      <li>Consent (no serious objections)</li>
      <li>Consensus (everyone agrees)</li>
      <li>Random decision (lot, coin toss, dice)</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm opacity-80">
      Each of these six options is better than letting decisions be made by
      whoever talks the loudest.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    When it's clear how decisions are made, confidence grows — especially in
    complex situations. Different perspectives and decision needs find their
    place without leading to paralysis or ambiguity.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 text-xl font-semibold">
    Role Clarity and Responsibility
  </h3>

  <p>
    Role conflicts often arise not from unwillingness but from uncertainty,
    ambiguity, or hidden expectations. Interventions in this area help teams
    name roles explicitly, make responsibilities visible, and clarify scope for
    action.
  </p>

  <p>
    Common approaches range from role cards to responsibility matrices to peer
    exchange on role perceptions. Often the discussion includes how implicit
    roles (mediator, critic, driver) operate, regardless of formal function.
  </p>

  <p>
    Role clarity creates space for individuality within a stable structure.
    Conscious reflection on tasks, responsibilities, and implicit expectations
    helps reduce tension and fosters understanding of how difference can be
    functionally integrated.
  </p>

  <h3 class="mt-8 mb-3 text-xl font-semibold">
    Conflict Resolution and Working with Tension
  </h3>

  <p>
    Tension is a natural part of teamwork, especially where difference is lived.
    Interventions in this area aim for early, structured, and respectful
    handling of conflict. This includes clarification formats, facilitation
    aids, conversation rules, and preventive agreements.
  </p>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <h3 class="text-info mb-2 font-semibold">
      Tip: Identifying conflict levels
    </h3>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-80">
      Knowing the conflict level helps choose the right approach:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-2 ml-5 list-disc space-y-1 text-sm opacity-80">
      <li><strong>Fact level:</strong> the issue / methods / goals</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Structure level:</strong> structures / distribution / roles
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Individual level:</strong> relationship / interests / values
      </li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    The key insight: the problem isn't the conflict — it's that it can't be
    discussed. A team that can address tensions early protects its relationships
    and uses conflict as an impetus for growth.
  </p>

  <p>
    Interventions in conflict handling enable teams not to avoid tensions but to
    actively work with them. Difference becomes not a risk but a developmental
    impulse. Conflicts become a sign of alive, differentiated collaboration.
  </p>

  <div class="tip-box">
    <h3 class="text-info mb-2 font-semibold">Tip: Making amends</h3>
    <p class="text-sm opacity-80">
      Some conflicts require making amends. Three simple-sounding elements make
      for successful amends:
    </p>
    <ol class="mt-2 ml-5 list-decimal space-y-1 text-sm opacity-80">
      <li>Name what happened or went wrong</li>
      <li>Take responsibility for your part in it</li>
      <li>Promise what you'll do differently or better in the future</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm opacity-80">
      Making amends should be practiced together — it's only a matter of time
      before it's needed.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>
